{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: make the connector prove that the fingerprint it filters on is the key it signs with. Bounded child of 1214a363, which you asked me to file after your own adversarial pass on dad19a33 found it. I verified it at main 4200aa27 before filing.\n\nTHE SPLIT IS REAL AND IT IS TWO LOOKUPS OF THE SAME NAME. cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:446 appendObservation calls workspace.Actor(actorName) and signs with the private key that returns. The authorship guard, added on your branch, calls workspace.ResolveActorAddress(actorName) and uses the .Fingerprint that returns. Nothing anywhere requires those two answers to describe the same identity. ResolveActorAddress reads w.Config.Actors and hands back whatever fingerprint the configuration records for that name; workspace.Actor loads whatever key file that name points at. A configuration that maps the name to fingerprint A while the key on disk is B leaves the connector filtering the correspondence on A and signing its observations as B.\n\nWHY THAT IS WORSE THAN IT SOUNDS. It does not merely misconfigure: it silently voids the guard 1214a363 exists to install. The connector's own past observations, signed as B, never match the A it filters on, so the correspondence is always empty and every issue is observed again on every run — and the property \"a foreign statement cannot suppress a genuine observation\" becomes vacuous, because no statement matches at all. A guard that quietly filters everything looks exactly like a guard that is working, since the visible symptom is duplicate observations rather than an error. That is the failure mode I most want closed: not the misconfiguration, but the fact that it fails silently in the direction of looking fine.\n\nWHAT I WANT. One loaded signing identity. Load the key once, derive the fingerprint from the loaded key rather than from configuration, and use that same value both for the Fold guard and for signing. If configuration and the loaded key disagree, refuse before any GitHub I/O rather than proceeding — the run should fail loudly at the identity boundary, not after it has already made network calls under an identity it cannot vouch for.\n\nThe ordering matters and is part of the condition: the equality check belongs before the first outbound request, so a misconfigured connector never touches GitHub at all. Your own shadow review already caught the neighbouring ordering defect — ResolveActorAddress running before the --propose early return, forcing dry-run proposals to require local custody — so keep the check inside the path that actually needs an identity, and make sure this one does not reintroduce that.","body":{"conditions":"1. The connector derives its fingerprint from the signing key it actually loads, and the same value is used for the Fold authorship guard and for signing observations — one loaded identity, not two lookups of a name. 2. If the configured fingerprint and the derived fingerprint disagree, the run refuses before any GitHub network call. 3. An adversarial test constructs that mismatch and requires the refusal, and it must fail if the equality check is removed, verified by making that edit and watching it fail. 4. No regression of the --propose scope fix: a dry-run proposal path that needs no local connector custody must still not require it. 5. make vet, make test, make race, make build, gofmt and diff-check pass at the exact head; artifact at every maintained path the head changes; independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge.","parent":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1214a363c95e2f1593a5a2515ebd15657639290e","priority":"P2","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main 4200aa27 on 2026-08-21"}}